# Three site blocks, one per public-facing surface. Every address below is a plain
# env-var placeholder ({$VAR}, Caddy's own syntax -- it does NOT support a bash-style
# `:-default` suffix; a literal `{$VAR:-:80}` gets parsed as env var "VAR:-:80", which
# doesn't exist, substituting to an empty string and producing a broken site block
# (confirmed with `caddy adapt`: it silently became a host-matcher on the literal
# string "-"). The actual default lives in docker-compose.yml's caddy service
# `environment:` block instead, using Compose's own `${VAR:-default}` substitution --
# by the time Caddy reads these, a real value is always already set. Caddy only
# attempts automatic HTTPS (a real ACME/Let's Encrypt certificate request) for a block
# whose address is an actual hostname, never for a bare ":port" address, so an
# unconfigured dev/shared box (docker-compose.yml's own defaults) gets plain HTTP on an
# internal port and Caddy never reaches out to Let's Encrypt at all. A real deployment
# sets these three vars (plus CADDY_HTTP_BIND/CADDY_HTTPS_BIND) in its own .env to the
# real public hostnames -- see infra/README.md's "Production box" section.
#
# Why three separate hostnames instead of path-routing everything under one domain: the
# product's user-FACING surface (the marketing site and the real app) is deliberately one
# origin (newton.best + newton.best/chat -- see apps/website/next.config.ts's own
# comment) -- but the API and Keycloak's hosted login page are never navigated to
# directly by a person, only fetched/redirected to by code, so a subdomain costs nothing
# in UX and avoids this Caddyfile having to path-strip "/api" off every FastAPI route
# (none of which are written to expect that prefix) or collide with apps/web's own
# "/chat" path. Auth uses Bearer tokens, not cookies (see app/core/security.py), so a
# separate subdomain has no CORS/SameSite cost either.

# Baseline security headers -- found missing entirely during the newton.best cutover's
# own security audit (checked with `curl -D -`: no HSTS, no X-Frame-Options, nothing).
# `(snippet)` + `import` so this applies identically to every real public site block
# rather than copy-pasted three times and drifting. X-Frame-Options DENY is safe here:
# nothing this product serves is meant to be embedded in another site's iframe.
(security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
	}
}

{$NEWTON_WEBSITE_ADDR} {
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy website:3000
}

# www redirects to the apex rather than being a second live copy of the same site --
# one canonical URL, no duplicate-content surface, no risk of the two ever silently
# drifting (a session cookie set on one not applying to the other, etc., even though
# this app doesn't use cookies today -- no reason to build in that failure mode for
# later). Deliberately its own site block/hostname rather than trying to fit two
# hostnames in NEWTON_WEBSITE_ADDR's one placeholder -- untested and unnecessary.
{$NEWTON_WWW_ADDR} {
	redir https://{$NEWTON_WEBSITE_ADDR}{uri} permanent
}

{$NEWTON_API_ADDR} {
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy api:8000
}

{$NEWTON_AUTH_ADDR} {
	import security_headers
	# The admin console has no legitimate public audience -- every real admin action
	# already goes over the existing SSH-tunnel pattern (infra/README.md's "Local
	# development against it", port 58180/58180-equivalent, loopback-only, never through
	# this public hostname) or `docker compose exec` directly against the container.
	# Leaving it reachable here is pure attack surface (credential stuffing against
	# KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD) for zero real benefit -- found during the newton.best
	# cutover's own security audit.
	@admin path /admin*
	respond @admin 404
	reverse_proxy keycloak:8080
}
